 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Vg500 Parallel Pipeline Architecture](2072-vg500-parallel-pipeline-architecture.md)
-A specific implementation using fixed-function hardware units for interpolation, shading, and compositing, distributing processing across four identical pipelines in a single ASIC.
+The Vg500 Parallel Pipeline Architecture is a highly parallel, template-based ray-casting hardware accelerator implemented as the vg500 ASIC for the VolumePro system. It solves the real-time rendering bottleneck by distributing the processing of adjacent voxel samples across four identical pipelines operating in parallel. Unlike general-purpose DSP-based solutions like VIRIM, which require multiple chips and lower frame rates for similar volumes, the Vg500 integrates the entire rendering pipeline into a single ASIC, enabling real-time frame rates for large datasets. It builds on the Time-Sliced Pipelined Architecture by utilizing fixed-function hardware units for interpolation, shading, and compositing.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Real-time performance is achieved by overlapping execution stages, effectively reducing the critical path per frame by parallelizing task execution [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the size of the context memory and slice buffers required to store intermediate results for time-sliced access [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High integration, component reusability, continuous hardware-software communication, and real-time interactivity [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Complexity in design hierarchy and layout; potential for resource contention in the context memory [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When real-time rendering of large volumetric datasets is required on dedicated hardware (ASIC/FPGA) and software flexibility is secondary to performance [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Enables real-time rendering by overlapping execution stages [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses context memory for inter-component communication, storing intermediate results for several clock cycles [S1, unofficial].
